.TH MUTIBLE 1 "November 2026" "mutible" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
mutible, mbl \- small editor for text, code, and LaTeX, with a browser and terminal
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B mutible
.RB [ \-\-foreground ]
.RI [ path ]
.br
.B mutible
.BR \-v " | " \-\-version
.br
.B mutible
.BR \-h " | " \-\-help
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B mutible
opens a window with a file tree on the left and an editor on the right.
.B mbl
is a shorter name for the same command.
.PP
If
.I path
is a folder, it becomes the root of the file tree. If it is a file, the
file is opened and the tree is rooted at the folder that holds it. With no
argument the current directory is opened.
.PP
mutible runs as a single instance. Running it again while it is open
passes the path to the running instance, which opens a new window on that
folder, or brings forward the window that already has it and shows the file
there, and the command returns at once.
.PP
Started from a terminal, the command returns as soon as the window is up,
and mutible runs apart from the terminal, as VS Code's
.B code
does: closing the terminal leaves mutible open, and the terminal gives no
warning about a running process. What mutible prints after that goes
nowhere, unless it was sent to a file. A problem before the window, such as
no display to open it on, is printed in the terminal, and the command exits
with mutible's status.
.PP
Ctrl+Shift+H shows every keyboard shortcut in the window.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-foreground
Stay in the terminal until mutible quits, as any program run there does,
with its output in the terminal. Closing the terminal then closes mutible.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print a summary of the usage and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-
End the options; what follows is a path, even when it starts with a dash.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/mutible/settings.conf
Colors, opacity, and language server commands. Ctrl+, creates it with every
setting commented out and opens it. It follows
.BR $XDG_CONFIG_HOME .
.TP
.I ~/.local/state/mutible
Sessions, recent folders, zoom, and the other state kept between runs. It
follows
.BR $XDG_STATE_HOME .
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/mutible/bin/tectonic
Where the LaTeX preview puts tectonic when it is asked to download it. It
follows
.BR $XDG_DATA_HOME .
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B MUTIBLE_FOREGROUND
Set to anything but an empty value or 0, the same as
.BR \-\-foreground ,
for scripts and debugging.
.TP
.B MUTIBLE_SETTINGS
A settings file to read in place of the one above.
.TP
.B MUTIBLE_TECTONIC
The tectonic binary the LaTeX preview uses, ahead of any other.
.TP
.B MUTIBLE_LSP_LOG
A file to append every language server message to, for debugging.
.TP
.B SHELL
The shell the terminal panel runs.
.PP
Each
.B MUTIBLE_
variable is also read under its name from before 1.6.0,
.BR MINICODE_FOREGROUND ,
.B MINICODE_SETTINGS
and so on, when the new name is not set.
.SH HISTORY
mutible was called minicode until 1.6.0. The
.B minicode
command still starts it, and says it is now mutible. On its first launch
mutible copies what minicode kept in
.IR ~/.config/minicode ,
.I ~/.local/state/minicode
and
.I ~/.local/share/minicode
to the folders above, when they do not exist yet, and leaves the old
folders as they were. A launcher pinned to the dock under the old name is
pinned again from the new one.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR git (1),
.BR clangd (1)
.PP
https://mut.ible.ai
